{"step_index":155,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:01:21Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:01:21+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:01:25+02:00\nThe search for \"\"weak Roman domination\" \"total domination\" \"\\gamma_t(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G)\" OR \"\\gamma_t \\le \\gamma_R\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, the relationship between domination parameters is a frequent subject of study[1][2][3]. While specific inequalities such as $\\gamma_t(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G)$ (where $\\gamma_t$ is the total domination number and $\\gamma_R$ is the Roman domination number) are often explored for various graph classes[4][2][5], the parameters you mentioned involve distinct definitions:\n\n*   **Total Domination Number ($\\gamma_t(G)$):** The minimum weight of a function $f: V \\to \\{0, 1\\}$ such that every vertex is adjacent to at least one vertex with $f(v) = 1$ (effectively, every vertex must be dominated by an adjacent vertex)[1][2].\n*   **Weak Roman Domination Number ($\\gamma_r(G)$ or $\\gamma_{wR}(G)$):** A variation where the function $f: V \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$ must satisfy specific conditions regarding the defense of vertices with weight 0[6].\n*   **Total Weak Roman Domination Number ($\\gamma_{tr}(G)$):** A concept that combines total domination and weak Roman domination[1].\n\nResearch typically establishes bounds by comparing these values to standard domination numbers ($\\gamma$) or between related variants[1][5][6]. If you are looking for a specific inequality or proof involving $\\gamma_t(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G)$, note that such relationships often depend on the structure of the graph (e.g., whether it is a tree, bipartite, or has specific degree constraints)[1][2][5]. \n\nIf you are investigating a specific conjecture or inequality, it may be helpful to check recent surveys on \"Total Roman Domination\" or \"Weak Roman Domination\" in journals such as *Discrete Applied Mathematics* or *Graphs and Combinatorics*, as these parameters are frequently updated with new bounds and characterizations[1][4][2].\n\nSources:\n[1] [mdpi.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF1uxddTMxuXxg1Sm8wbYs7b3MZrU6atJrLRoHeFZcHNq8pCjiHA_7V6i7xmrL5i0WcZ-WMWRaH3wsGmxHG57AwxzMQ03fTDlx9wT6D_t4j3N4WlTcCLqszlCZEKR0=)\n[2] [isikun.edu.tr](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHu1owIHOYHmfmmhwf6e6WEJOqj2TLdi2LPEMEAXN6wZD9GQ5pBJtNUMVLBclgJ2EKOztNJ4OkQKLx4EZcoPJx_ukO70HXrFoZmcGtD8ASKPhejk6BfhXYXcD73YXPl9gBjV-3pbc6EoQZjJhjmNt-oZfV-y-MR5g==)\n[3] [youtube.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFuhKfA4Hch9fkspzzznLS0OL7IxKl6YKUAJrdGfWZeXl9khbzToSsJ6j8X41Fphw88sqRbeilmbwC8QH_nNuO88BIByZ01ODnN2R--mUI8LoOd8j2LVe-JpHjgPrsvDsY_)\n[4] [bg.ac.rs](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFxnJx3XUICLC1WyE5UJpf_1-kNlxEKXF_Q10oTDBvXqAvAEI4kBJ0HmcSlJaR_gbi6PAVUVUOuP95jUG181Eg4rlZAqKHvFVd06eVPR3L7FbKB1jtDZ91CSfWhUwyRgSN-Gy_hQENo)\n[5] [ijsdr.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHY7etYUYS-b5xHQfF0Gxw9IdWEYM6FVF0Qt0Zn-VvE0v9uO-QuZPq9v0qvaCJsxWpcgGOhsbHyeb32VD2Z5aK_NKu3B9kuTKAFAnt15FwjvseOhJc-zeQKFo7nH4N8QpG4_c4=)\n[6] [ijmsi.ir](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQELKu0K5A1jsWikT-XeaZYFs3QbXjG8kchzM7gAj7l3qkvS92slJtPYAiVBfSUhnspdoH5Ut7decrXWS-5m--y4gMymNWtIAObLG5CgvOD33JCHi31sdi-8kZzP8QoNkCTIi4TyS4TXM7Kus-p9DmxejrAtLw==)"}
{"step_index":156,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:01:25Z","thinking":"**Exploring Relationships Between Domination Numbers**\n\nDirect proof of the target inequality might be difficult; considering relationships with the standard domination number. The Roman domination number has known bounds concerning the standard domination number: $\\gamma(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G) \\le 2\\gamma(G)$, per established literature. The relationship between the total domination number and standard domination number is similar: $\\gamma(G) \\le \\gamma_t(G) \\le 2\\gamma(G)$.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"\\\"graphs with\\\" \\\"\\\\gamma_t = 2\\\\gamma\\\" OR \\\"\\\\gamma_t(G) = 2 \\\\gamma(G)\\\" OR \\\"\\\\gamma_t(G) = 2\\\\gamma(G)\\\"\"","toolAction":"\"Searching for graphs with gamma_t = 2 gamma\"","toolSummary":"\"Search for graphs with gamma_t = 2 gamma\""}}]}
{"step_index":157,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:01:28Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:01:28+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:01:31+02:00\nThe search for \"\"graphs with\" \"\\gamma_t = 2\\gamma\" OR \"\\gamma_t(G) = 2 \\gamma(G)\" OR \"\\gamma_t(G) = 2\\gamma(G)\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe equality $\\gamma_t(G) = 2\\gamma(G)$ relates the **total domination number** ($\\gamma_t$) and the **domination number** ($\\gamma$) of a graph $G$.\n\nWhile there is no single, universally famous class of graphs defined exclusively by this specific equality, the relationship between these two parameters is a central topic in domination theory[1].\n\n### Context and Related Concepts\n\n*   **General Relationship:** For any graph $G$ with no isolated vertices, it is well-established that $\\gamma(G) \\le \\gamma_t(G) \\le 2\\gamma(G)$. The upper bound $\\gamma_t(G) \\le 2\\gamma(G)$ is a classic result in the field[1].\n*   **Equality $\\gamma_t(G) = 2\\gamma(G)$:** Graphs attaining this upper bound are of interest in extremal graph theory. Research in this area often seeks to characterize graphs that satisfy equality for various domination-type parameters[2][3].\n*   **Related Studies:**\n    *   **Equal Parameters:** Much research focuses on graphs where $\\gamma(G) = \\gamma_t(G)$[1].\n    *   **Specific Classes:** Researchers frequently investigate these parameters for specific families of graphs, such as trees[4], hypercubes[5], or regular graphs[6].\n    *   **Extremal Characterizations:** Studies often characterize graphs where certain domination numbers equal small integers (like 2) or specific functions of the order of the graph ($n$)[2][3][7].\n\nIf you are looking for a specific paper or a structural characterization of graphs where $\\gamma_t(G) = 2\\gamma(G)$, it is common to find these results in journals specializing in combinatorics and graph theory (e.g., *Discrete Mathematics*, *Journal of Graph Theory*). You may want to search for \"graphs satisfying the equality of total domination and twice the domination number\" to find the most recent characterizations.\n\nSources:\n[1] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGZGeefjqTMr5_AMzL8kax3_4RCK1e0kwYb48NTeY1f_G9CXSr1Bmkd7UpwOkitVYe9ymKyjJMQowaHxqVAZaLmixh7MiHXqFNbQcSsRvjJQFA-GvcXRAeeQWiMqLe_8t5y9GsFUqgaWJ1g91QVSBrR7eQNwmKPaheMSL3_61SxlbhSGJLjQTli-uAXNgwYD8Y4QH_wQEDaohLdwfNicGvFiXAKANA=)\n[2] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHEmAk4zNa1u5sA13yLk2cjYFgfjZvoEsyClRF5TxCOHq8bTQM7UCcdyoa2JuXCinz-3nUqsS2mqbQ6jHu64cm1FQmvu0InOkniU0o65Nt9pAAqB3rU6x7N1Q==)\n[3] [kexuetongbao-csb.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHnIEYr8JPr2tgz3LgVH0Nr_oa8mIUFSB6P26QtzMiFegtqw_xQLqh9d5FapUT7ndxRSFGaIt-D6jVjbk9QtnzljlK2qhr5NxuA9F0ZRyFHw_7JJDH2pG-rQwJV_lgqaod0-x6koFaseI93fgv7RVpuQNZc6-rWokArz6FKkRHRCIPDnaUIFmTURbxVlmeU5IFMzQ==)\n[4] [mdpi.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGr5xNB1rTK4EbGb6B95hp1QEQaWm3G0lkcA2Pfnm6VWfAFih2kOg40DzekihMtxWw8okDYsTbOy642Jq4Ujj3hyfD2JY-Y4or3P7Sc8QYExk4j5P8fwCzK77KoTcmLng==)\n[5] [uni-lj.si](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFVZtD1pX_xEIWyo0mFSwMSRQi9MGepBvmAeVBeZ6Ir2vLt4AycFLl4gSZj2xcpyB_ez_02vO_06QZ0h6MfLlMLhK_z8DnUxAuWKLHQ4EBhO5FMoYlbTo0EEQRx2rr2Ml637BhfVMcaCt5dJjZ6URS_YA11dy_f6UKuNMUrKQo=)\n[6] [ui.ac.ir](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH4lFpMpqq8dfhXNnSlOqBBel_fHrUA7IY9Abn-daG2WIR5yYT2vBj3zOsrkY8pcox6eNCEoU7bUy7uhu8wvi1oH96B1zO7BcLO6h_oRHy7OrPZFD0b0FI7BRtn)\n[7] [aimspress.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHwSsNzjgu6njPnAe_BFTRPu2PhKXN5LgSN_W_2mcQAKBKk7jUo54La0ORzcqtIjHMEHA4FpFA9aowOuehvqivKtjWNW5sr776UoKM10HE-rX7dQNjOl6qnpL81s5cIsmRGMoj60SMSKmEmihjuK4sVatvcx217uLsUYEcnQg==)"}
{"step_index":158,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:01:31Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c '\\nimport networkx as nx\\nimport itertools\\n\\n# Check small graphs for gamma, gamma_t, gamma_R\\nfor n in range(4, 9):\\n    for G in nx.graph_atlas_g():\\n        if G.number_of_nodes() != n or any(G.degree(v) == 0 for v in G.nodes()):\\n            continue\\n        nodes = list(G.nodes())\\n        adj = {u: set(G.neighbors(u)) for u in nodes}\\n        # gamma: min dominating set (v dominates itself and neighbors)\\n        gamma = None\\n        for k in range(1, n + 1):\\n            for S in itertools.combinations(nodes, k):\\n                dom = set(S)\\n                for u in S: dom.update(adj[u])\\n                if len(dom) == n:\\n                    gamma = k\\n                    break\\n            if gamma is not None: break\\n            \\n        # gamma_t\\n        gamma_t = None\\n        for k in range(2, n + 1):\\n            for S in itertools.combinations(nodes, k):\\n                if all(adj[u] & set(S) for u in nodes):\\n                    gamma_t = k\\n                    break\\n            if gamma_t is not None: break\\n            \\n        if gamma_t == 2 * gamma:\\n            # check gamma_R\\n            # gamma_R <= 2*gamma always!\\n            # Could gamma_R < gamma_t = 2*gamma?!\\n            # Compute gamma_R\\n            gamma_R = 2 * n\\n            for V2_mask in range(1 << n):\\n                k2 = bin(V2_mask).count(\\\"1\\\")\\n                k1 = 0\\n                for u in range(n):\\n                    if not (V2_mask & (1 << u)):\\n                        if (adj[u] & V2_mask) == 0:\\n                            k1 += 1\\n                w = 2 * k2 + k1\\n                if w < gamma_R: gamma_R = w\\n            print(f\\\"Graph with gamma_t = 2*gamma: n={n}, gamma={gamma}, gamma_t={gamma_t}, gamma_R={gamma_R}\\\")\\n            if gamma_R < gamma_t:\\n                print(\\\"COUNTEREXAMPLE!\\\")\\n                exit(0)\\n'\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_40a99333\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Checking graphs with gamma_t = 2 gamma\"","toolSummary":"\"Test gamma_t = 2 gamma graphs\""}}]}
